WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.866 --> 00:00:01.500
- Hey, gang.

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:02.969
Dave Baker.

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:04.303
Today, I want to take a
look at the weapons wielded

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:06.005
by the strongest
warriors that ever

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:11.978
lived in honor of
History's new show,

00:00:12.044 --> 00:00:13.646
"The Strongest Man in History,"

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:16.949
I'm pretty sure these guys could
handle these weapons, probably

00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:17.784
with one in either hand.

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:27.227
[rock music]

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.897
This monster of a blade
is the Scottish Claymore.

00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:33.266
Now the Scottish
Claymore may be the most

00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:35.335
iconic long sword in history.

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:38.804
The name Claymore comes
from the Gaelic Claidheamh

00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:40.706
Mor, which means "great sword."

00:00:40.806 --> 00:00:42.275
And great sword it is.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:46.078 line:0%
At about 5 feet in length, 4
feet of which is the blade,

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:48.715 line:0%
it was such a large weapon
that the average soldier

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:51.851 line:0%
had to use two
hands to wield it,

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.887 line:0%
making it a fearsome,
intimidating weapon

00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:56.121 line:7%
on the battlefield.

00:00:56.221 --> 00:00:58.358
The weapon was first
introduced in the 1500s

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:01.226
and used extensively by
Scottish Highland warriors

00:01:01.326 --> 00:01:03.228
all the way through the 1700s.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.164
But the Highland
warriors were such

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:07.700
a fearsome and deadly
group that even the Vikings

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.202
were intimidated by them.

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:12.138
This was a powerful weapon
wielded by powerful warriors.

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:25.484
This massive and intimidating
sword is the Zweihander.

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:28.087
Now, the design of this
blade, with its parrying

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:31.157
lugs and large guard and
the wave of the blade,

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:34.827
was used extensively
in pike battles.

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:38.465 line:0%
The guard could be used to
push pikes up out of the way.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:44.003
And the waves of the
blades could be used

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:47.673
when smashing down on pikes.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.041
It wouldn't slide off.

00:01:49.141 --> 00:01:51.410
It would actually catch
and break the pikes.

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:55.047 line:0%
Up to 6 feet long, weighing
up to 8 pounds, this

00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:57.483 line:0%
was the weapon of choice
for the elite guards

00:01:57.583 --> 00:02:01.454 line:0%
of the Landsknechte mercenaries,
German mercenaries who fought

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:04.524 line:0%
throughout Europe and were
some of the finest foot

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:08.093 line:0%
soldiers of their time,
notably enlisted by the future

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.630 line:0%
Holy Roman Emperor,
Maximilian I.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:14.033
They helped him with his rise to
power in the late 15th century.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:16.236 line:0%
Now, the Landsknechte
used these swords

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:21.273 line:0%
extensively in the Italian wars
from the 1490s to the 1550s.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.810
Now these weapons were
not used by small guys.

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:25.378
These were used
by large warriors

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:28.515
with the power to fight all day
long with a weapon this size.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.654
[rock music]

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:38.525
 How come?

00:02:42.728 --> 00:02:45.431
 Now this iconic
weapon is the Nagamaki.

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:47.934
The word "nagamaki"
roughly translates

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.304
to long wrap, which is one of
the features of this weapon.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.805
It's long-handled.

00:02:52.905 --> 00:02:57.376 line:0%
The blades could be roughly 3
feet long with an equal length

00:02:57.476 --> 00:03:00.213 line:0%
of handle wrapped in
leather or silk cord,

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.850 line:0%
giving it a lot of leverage
and action for the blade.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.287
Nice.

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:10.222
Now, it's hard to pinpoint the
exact origins of the Nagamaki.

00:03:10.322 --> 00:03:14.694
Some references mention
it in 794 to 1185,

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:18.564
where it would have been used
by Japan's deadliest warriors--

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:20.333
the Samurai.

00:03:20.399 --> 00:03:23.902
You can see that it's a
versatile, light, fast cutting

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:24.970
weapon.

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:26.606
Truly one of my favorites.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.852
[rock music]

00:03:39.953 --> 00:03:41.287
Now, this deadly
piece of business

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:44.289
is the knightly poleaxe,
favorite of armored knights

00:03:44.356 --> 00:03:45.658
from the 15th century.

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:47.793 line:0%
Now with its three
deadly features--

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:52.165 line:0%
an axe head; the chop;
a thrusting point

00:03:52.265 --> 00:03:54.667 line:0%
for taking a man off a horse
or a man in front of you

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:59.472 line:0%
on ground; and for
armor, a hammerhead.

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:04.711
You can see that the
shaft is steel-reinforced

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:06.312
and actually has a guard.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:08.914
That way it can be used to
parry weapons without damaging

00:04:09.014 --> 00:04:10.483
the shaft of the weapon.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:13.486 line:0%
Now you can see these weapons
in pictures and wood cuttings

00:04:13.552 --> 00:04:17.056 line:0%
from the 15th century being used
not only on the battlefield,

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:18.825 line:7%
but in judicial duels--

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:20.493 line:7%
trial by combat, if you will.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.929
Now this was a weapon
that respects no armor.

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:26.799
It was used by knights who
had both strength and skill.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.107
 That's pretty cool.

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:35.808
 Yes.

00:04:38.677 --> 00:04:40.379
 Now, this intimidating
piece of equipment

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:41.747
is the glaive guisarme.

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:45.217
Now, glaives were a popular
weapon from the 13th

00:04:45.284 --> 00:04:46.618
and 14th centuries.

00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:48.320
Originating in France,
they were usually

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:51.257
a single-edged blade
mounted on a pole, sometimes

00:04:51.323 --> 00:04:53.359
a knife blade or a sword blade.

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:56.596
But the glaive guisarme added
this sort of crescent hook

00:04:56.663 --> 00:04:57.930
onto the back.

00:04:57.996 --> 00:05:00.099 line:0%
Now, that allowed a warrior to
either hook someone and pull

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:02.201 line:0%
them towards them,
or to push someone

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:05.437 line:0%
away, or push a ladder
off a castle wall

00:05:05.537 --> 00:05:08.307 line:7%
or lift pikes out of the way.

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:09.775
And you can see the
size of the blade

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:13.313
was enlarged to make a
very large cutting surface.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:19.751
 That was brutal, dude.

00:05:19.818 --> 00:05:23.122
 Now, these blades
also had a long socket

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:27.659
and metal langet that ran
down the shaft of the weapon.

00:05:27.726 --> 00:05:29.495
Now, that kept that
shaft from being

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:32.598
cut or broken by swords,
axes, other weapons being

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:33.399
swung at it.

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:38.171
You can see in the
hands of a professional,

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.106
that is an incredibly
intimidating weapon.

00:05:45.177 --> 00:05:48.081
 That was brutal.

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:49.415
 So there you go.

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:50.816
Some of the deadliest
weapons on "Forged in Fire"

00:05:50.916 --> 00:05:52.851
used by the strongest
warriors in history.

00:05:52.951 --> 00:05:55.355
And don't forget to check
out History's newest show,

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:58.323
"The Strongest Man in
History" Wednesday 10:00/9:00

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:01.894
Central here on History.

